<%#
  008 FR-003: the stacked lockup — mark over wordmark over tagline — for the
  full-brand screens, which are sign-in and sign-up.

  These two screens use the supplied artwork as a raster image. The master is
  kept untouched at brand/brand-mark-source.png (1448x1086, 500KB, byte-for-byte
  what was handed over); what ships is a straight downscale of it to 800x600,
  which is still well over twice the largest size it is ever drawn at and is
  indistinguishable from the master at that size, while costing 59KB instead of
  500KB. The vector trace in shared/_brand_mark is what the header and the icons
  use, where the mark is small and has to recolour.

  008 FR-003a (superseded by 025): the tagline used to be hardcoded in English
  "so it matches the interface", with the source artwork's own French original
  ("Trouvez le casier qui vous convient") deliberately kept off every page. 025
  made the interface itself bilingual, which is exactly the premise FR-003a's
  rule rested on — so the tagline now follows the site language like every
  other string (t(".tagline")) instead of being pinned to English, and the
  artwork's own French phrase is what fr.yml uses. This is a text change only;
  FR-015's "no new control or behaviour" still holds.

  008 FR-022: the logo fades up out of a blur once.

  011 FR-001: and then keeps breathing — a slow opacity pulse that starts where
  that entrance ends and runs for as long as the screen is up, so the brand is
  what catches the eye on the screen a visitor meets first.
%>
<div class="brand-stacked">
  <%# Deliberately large here: sign-in and sign-up are the full-brand screens,
      and the mark is the thing a visitor should meet first. The stylesheet
      scales it down fluidly on a narrow screen.

      alt is empty on purpose — the wordmark right below it already names the
      brand, so a screen reader announcing the image too would just repeat it.

      width and height are the file's own, so the box is reserved before the
      image arrives and nothing jumps when it does. %>
  <%# fetchpriority high because this is the hero of the page: it should be
      fetched ahead of anything else the browser finds. %>
  <%# 024: the same clockwise spin as the header mark, on a wrapping span
      rather than the image itself — the image already owns the entrance's
      scale(), and a second transform animation on the same element would
      replace it outright rather than combine with it (see the header
      lockup's own comment on this). The delay before it starts is added in
      the stylesheet, scoped to this screen, so the spin waits out the
      entrance the same way the pulse already does. %>
  <span class="brand-mark-spin">
    <%= image_tag "brand-mark.png",
          alt: "",
          width: 800,
          height: 600,
          fetchpriority: "high",
          data: { brand_mark: true },
          class: "brand-mark brand-mark-flourish brand-mark-loop" %>
  </span>

  <p class="brand-wordmark brand-wordmark-display"><span class="brand-wordmark-lock">Lock</span><span class="brand-wordmark-swap">Swap</span></p>

  <p class="brand-tagline"><%= t(".tagline") %></p>
</div>
